Subject: the common browser shortcuts: Ctrl-w, Alt-Left/Right, Ctrl-Tab, zoom
 keys, F6

Someone at a Walnut console has used Firefox or Chrome, not upstream's own scheme: Ctrl-w closes the
tab (with Ctrl-F4), Alt-Left and Alt-Right go back and forward (upstream: previous and next tab;
Ctrl-[ and Ctrl-] still work), Ctrl-Tab and Ctrl-Shift-Tab change tabs (with Ctrl-PageUp/Down and
Alt-n), Alt-d and F6 go to the location bar (upstream's Alt-d closed the tab, a surprise to anyone
used to it focusing the address), Alt-Home opens the start page, Ctrl-+ and Ctrl-- zoom. The man
page says so.

diff --git a/badwolf.1 b/badwolf.1
--- a/badwolf.1
+++ b/badwolf.1
@@ -44,12 +44,16 @@ Resets webpage zoom to 100%.
 Opens the selected link in a new tab. (Note: JS still overrides the event)
 .It any Ctrl-t
 Creates a new tab (in a new session, similar as pressing the button)
-.It browser Ctrl-F4, browser Alt-d
+.It browser Ctrl-w, browser Ctrl-F4
 Closes the current tab
 .It browser Ctrl-f
 Focuses on the search entry
-.It browser Ctrl-l
+.It browser Ctrl-l, browser Alt-d, browser F6
 Focuses on the location(URL) entry
+.It browser Alt-Home
+Opens the start page
+.It browser Ctrl-+ / Ctrl--
+Zooms the webpage in/out.
 .It browser Ctrl-Shift-r / Ctrl-r, browser F5
 Reloads the content in the current tab (with/without clearing cache)
 .It browser Escape
@@ -60,12 +64,12 @@ Terminates the web process
 Toggles caret browsing.
 .It browser F12
 Opens the web inspector.
-.It browser Ctrl-[ / Ctrl-]
+.It browser Ctrl-[ / Ctrl-], browser Alt-Left / Alt-Right
 Go back/forward in current tab's history
 .It browser Ctrl-p
 Print the current page. (spawns a dialog)
-.It any Alt-Left / Alt-Right
-Go to the previous/next tab
+.It any Ctrl-Tab / Ctrl-Shift-Tab
+Go to the next/previous tab
 .It any F1
 Shows the about dialog
 .It any Alt-n
